import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as i}from"./chunks/framework.BUOFcz_J.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Rho execution bridge","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"concepts/execution.md","filePath":"concepts/execution.md"}'),s={name:"concepts/execution.md"};function r(n,e,d,c,h,l){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<h1 id="rho-execution-bridge" tabindex="-1">Rho execution bridge <a class="header-anchor" href="#rho-execution-bridge" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Rho execution bridge&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This bridge covers explicitly selected managed operations and Host activity. Agents normally use their native tools under existing permissions. Their native work does not need a Core Host or a managed operation record. Activity observation can retain selected external evidence without taking over execution. Existing managed records keep the identity, continuity and honest facts described below.</p><p>Rho reaches <a href="https://github.com/YuLab-SMU/Rho-core" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Rho-core</a> only through its documented public CLI. Core owns the managed execution contract and the resident Host. The first-release local Rho service invokes that CLI; the browser WebUI uses the service&#39;s scoped application operations. This document owns the application side of that boundary: request identity, the exit-code state machine, Host lifetime and bounded observation. The <a href="/Rho/docs/records/backend-first">backend plan</a> selects versioned HTTP JSON and SSE for the application service; concrete authentication and library versions remain to be implemented under the <a href="/Rho/docs/concepts/techstack#local-service-and-workspace">technology boundary</a>.</p><p><a href="/Rho/docs/concepts/architecture">Architecture</a> defines ownership; <a href="/Rho/docs/reference/status">Status</a> records implementation; <a href="/Rho/docs/concepts/roadmap">Roadmap</a> selects the flow that exercises it. The authoritative Core documents are <a href="https://github.com/YuLab-SMU/Rho-core/blob/main/docs/MANAGED-RUN.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Managed run</a> and <a href="https://github.com/YuLab-SMU/Rho-core/blob/main/docs/HOST-CLI.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Host CLI</a>. Where this document and Core differ, Core&#39;s contract governs.</p><h2 id="request-identity" tabindex="-1">Request identity <a class="header-anchor" href="#request-identity" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Request identity&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An identity is a caller name plus a request id. Each is 1–128 characters from <code>[A-Za-z0-9._:@-]</code>.</p><ul><li>Rho mints the request id once, before the first submit of a logical action, and persists it with that action&#39;s record.</li><li>The identity names one request for one caller. Submitting the same content again returns the original run and dispatches nothing; different content under the same identity is a conflict.</li><li>A new identity is created only for deliberately new work. A lost reply, a wait timeout, browser refresh, view crash, service crash or reconnect never mints one.</li><li>The caller name is an application namespace, not a user or a security principal. Core caller namespaces are not authenticated.</li></ul><h2 id="exit-codes" tabindex="-1">Exit codes <a class="header-anchor" href="#exit-codes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Exit codes&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each CLI call maps to an explicit application state. This mapping is the single place where &quot;do not replay&quot; becomes mechanical rather than a policy sentence.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Rho action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>The Host executed the request</td><td>Read <code>value</code>; after <code>wait</code> the run may still be active</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>The Host answered an error, or an over-limit request was not sent</td><td>Act on <code>error.kind</code> and <code>message</code>; do not resend unchanged</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Usage error, nothing sent</td><td>Application defect; surface it</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Not delivered: no Host, Host busy, refused or protocol mismatch; no effect</td><td>Retry later with the same identity; starting a Host is a separate authorized lifecycle action</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Sent but no reply; the request may have executed</td><td><code>lookup</code> the same identity, or resend the same identity; never a new one</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Code 4 is the reconnection case the product must show honestly: the task may exist. Rho queries the original identity and displays the actual facts, including an unknown outcome, instead of starting fresh work.</p><h2 id="host-lifetime" tabindex="-1">Host lifetime <a class="header-anchor" href="#host-lifetime" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Host lifetime&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The local Rho service owns the lifecycle of Hosts it starts. An existing Host retains its original owner. No browser tab, view, window or Agent session owns a Host, and none of their lifetimes is a reason to stop or restart it.</p><ul><li>At service startup Rho uses the public CLI&#39;s <code>capability</code> command to observe a Host at <code>state_dir/host.sock</code> and check its project. An existing Host is reused without transferring lifecycle ownership. A second Host on the same <code>state_dir</code> would fail at the storage lock, so Rho does not start one.</li><li>If no Host exists, an authorized service/project start can start <code>rho-core serve --project ... --state-dir ...</code>, read the single <code>ready</code> JSON line and hold the child process. A browser observation or reconnect alone does not start a Host or recover a native session.</li><li>The state directory is <code>.rho/</code> inside the project, already listed in <code>.gitignore</code>. One Host serves one project. An existing Host on a project is adopted, never replaced.</li><li>On explicit service exit Rho sends SIGTERM only to a Host it started and reads that child&#39;s <code>shutdown</code> report. It releases observation of an existing Host without stopping it; stopping that Host requires existing authorization covering its shutdown. Host shutdown, task cancellation and confirmed native stop stay separate operations with separate effects in the interface.</li><li>Browser close, refresh, connection loss and view failure release observation and rendering only. They do not stop the local service or Host, cancel a task or restart an R session. A later desktop client&#39;s view closure has the same boundary.</li><li>A service crash is not a confirmed Host shutdown or native stop. Preserve the original request identity and any partial/unknown outcome. After a service restart, inspect the existing Host and original request through the public CLI; do not automatically repeat execution or claim native-session recovery.</li><li>On Windows a Host serves a named pipe named from a hash of its state folder, known from its <code>ready</code> line or found among Core&#39;s pipes by the project its <code>capability</code> names. Node there ends the processes it starts when it ends, so <code>cmd.exe</code> starts the Host and waits for it; the Host outlives the service and its runs share cmd&#39;s console, which has no window. A Host takes Ctrl+Break as an end without its report, so Rho starts it with <code>RHO_CORE_STOP_FILE</code> and creates that file instead of sending SIGTERM. A Host serves one pipe instance at a time; a call that finds it busy is not delivered (exit 3) and is tried up to five times (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/windows-0-5-2-episode">Windows episode</a>).</li></ul><p>Reads are bounded observations. A read never starts or recovers a Host or a native session.</p><h2 id="bounded-observation" tabindex="-1">Bounded observation <a class="header-anchor" href="#bounded-observation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Bounded observation&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Rho renders only what Core retained. <code>read</code> returns <code>offset</code>, <code>next_offset</code>, <code>more_retained</code> and <code>lossy</code>; the application keeps them so a truncated or lossy stream is visible rather than silently completed. Retained process output is an execution observation, not a scientific artifact. Scientific artifacts are ordinary files that the component owns, with its own manifest and checks.</p><p>Execution completion, artifact integrity, domain checking and model explanation are separate facts and remain separately visible.</p>',18)])])}const m=t(s,[["render",r]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
